WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.880
Hey guys, quick note before we get into the&nbsp;
video. I've been pretty busy moving between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.640
states and working on my startup, Silo. So, my&nbsp;
brother is filling in for me on this video. Let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.440
me know what you think in the comments. With&nbsp;
that out of the way, let's get right into it.&nbsp;

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.080
Nobody’s using Tinder.
Which is kind of crazy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:24.400
because basically every dating app copied it.
The swipe, the match, super-like, all of it.&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.920
Its parent company has even fallen from&nbsp;
over $45 billion in value to just 8.&nbsp;

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.000
Tinder’s paying users have dropped&nbsp;
every single year since 2022.&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.720
Last year, its revenue fell&nbsp;
for the first time ever.&nbsp;

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:42.320
And now the app is even getting rid of the iconic&nbsp;
swipe and using AI to look through your photos to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.560
figure out who you should match with.
But that’s not even the weird part.&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.640
Behind the scenes, there’s been this&nbsp;
huge fight over Tinder for years.&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.760
Nine different people have been in charge of&nbsp;
it. And at one point, its own parent company was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.880
actually trying to push Tinder’s value down.
Somehow, it gets even weirder.&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.640
Because the new dating app that’s&nbsp;
quickly taking its place is owned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:13.120
by the exact same company.
So, what happened to Tinder?&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.040
It’s 2011, and dating in&nbsp;
real life isn’t going great.&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:26.400
But online dating was pretty bad too.
You’d answer a bunch of questions, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.760
website would basically turn you into a score.
Some sites would even tell you exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.280
how many people had ignored you.
So Sean Rad and engineer Joe Munoz wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.840
to make the whole thing a lot simpler.
[Sean Rad] “On Tinder, there are no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.880
questionnaires to fill out. When was the last&nbsp;
time you walked into a bar and someone said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.600
‘Excuse me, can you fill out this form&nbsp;
and we’ll match you up with people here?’”&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.720
Thus, Tinder was born.
It basically turned dating into a game.&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:02.062
Each person showed up like a card. You’d&nbsp;
swipe left to pass or right if you liked them.&nbsp;

00:02:02.062 --> 00:02:06.720
[Jonathan Badeen] “When I stepped out, the room&nbsp;
was especially foggy. I wiped the mirror clean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.640
but within a minute it was fogging up again. I&nbsp;
wiped it clean a second time, only this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.080
I wiped in the opposite direction. I saw&nbsp;
a familiar face looking back at me in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:23.120
clear sliver of the mirror that my hand had&nbsp;
just … swiped. The swipe was the simplest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:30.480
way to get the card from point A to point B.”
Yes, really. That’s how the swipe was invented.&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:36.080
But the reality is more nefarious.
The animations, sounds, and little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.480
celebration when you got a match made&nbsp;
it feel like you had won something.&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.800
And the foggy mirror was only part of the story.
According to Badeen, the matching system was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.560
based on something called&nbsp;
a variable-ratio schedule.&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.320
You never know which swipe&nbsp;
will hit, so you keep going.&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.480
One swipe gives you nothing. But&nbsp;
the next one might give you a match.&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.280
So you swipe again because&nbsp;
maybe the next one is the one.&nbsp;

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:07.760
[Badeen] "We have some of these almost game-like&nbsp;
elements. It kinda works like a slot machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.720
You're excited to see who the next person is&nbsp;
— or excited to see, did I get the match?"&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.840
And weirdly enough, it was partly inspired&nbsp;
by B.F. Skinner’s pigeon experiments from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.920
the 1940s. Which I’de recommend reading.
Even TIME ran an article called “Meet the Guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.640
Who Turned Dating Into an Addiction.”
But clearly, it was working.&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.920
Just two years after launch, people were&nbsp;
opening Tinder 11 times a day and spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:39.920
around 90 minutes on it. Every single&nbsp;
day, users were swiping a billion times.&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:45.200
And pretty quickly, Tinder became the&nbsp;
dating app that wasn’t embarrassing to use.&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:51.360
By 2013, it was even taking off in places like&nbsp;
Dubai and Turkey, with more than 20,000 new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.880
users signing up every day.
They even won TechCrunch’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.880 --> 00:04:00.960
Best New Startup of 2013.
But there was still one pretty big problem.&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.600
How were they actually going to make money?
Three years after launch, Tinder finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.400
figured out how to make money from the&nbsp;
thing people couldn’t stop doing: swiping.&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:16.480
For the first time, free users could only&nbsp;
swipe so many times. But with Tinder Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:21.200
you could swipe as much as you wanted&nbsp;
and even undo an accidental left swipe.&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.640
You could also hide your&nbsp;
profile with Incognito Mode.&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:30.400
Then in 2016, Tinder launched Boost.
For a few dollars, Tinder would push your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.760
profile to the front so more people would see you.
A year later, Tinder Gold let you see everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.520
who already liked you. And you&nbsp;
got a free Boost every month.&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:45.200
Within weeks, Tinder became the highest-grossing&nbsp;
app on the App Store, even beating Netflix.&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.680
And the money started pouring in.
Revenue went from $47 million in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:56.880
2015 to $169 million in 2016. By&nbsp;
2019, it had crossed $1 billion.&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:03.920
By then, Tinder alone was making up 58% of&nbsp;
its parent company Match Group’s revenue.&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:07.280
But some of the ways they made&nbsp;
money were a little weird.&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:12.720
Imagine you’re 31 and decide to buy Tinder Plus.
You pay $19.99 a month.&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.840
Then you find out someone who’s 29 is&nbsp;
paying just $9.99 for the exact same thing.&nbsp;

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:21.680
Almost half the price.
Tinder said ‘During our testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.720
we've learned, not surprisingly, that younger&nbsp;
users are just as excited about Tinder Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.360
but are more budget constrained, and&nbsp;
need a lower price to pull the trigger,’&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:36.800
That price difference eventually led to a&nbsp;
class-action lawsuit over age discrimination.&nbsp;

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:40.160
But for now, it was working.
Everything was going great.&nbsp;

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:44.880
You only found out about the people who liked&nbsp;
you back. Everyone else just disappeared.&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.720
And somehow, swiping started&nbsp;
to feel almost like gambling.&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.440
Which makes sense because&nbsp;
it was designed that way.&nbsp;

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.400
But before we get to that, we&nbsp;
need to talk about the weird fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.400 --> 00:06:03.600
happening behind the scenes to control Tinder.
Because that fight may have helped ruin the app.&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:13.520
Remember who actually owned Tinder?
Match Group. And at the time, IAC too.&nbsp;

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.760
Well, Tinder co-founder Sean Rad&nbsp;
and nine other former executives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:23.200
sued both companies. They claimed Match&nbsp;
and IAC purposely pushed Tinder’s value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:26.640
down to just $3 billion.
Here’s what happened:&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:32.000
Sean Rad was out as CEO in March&nbsp;
2015 and replaced by Chris Payne.&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.360
Only for Rad to come back five months later&nbsp;
before stepping down again in December 2016.&nbsp;

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:43.280
Then Match CEO Greg Blatt took over.
Not long after, Match Group and IAC tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:48.800
to buy out Tinder’s founders. And they&nbsp;
said Tinder was worth just $3 billion.&nbsp;

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:54.320
The founders said it was actually worth $13&nbsp;
billion. And they had a pretty good reason why.&nbsp;

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:57.920
Tinder had started launching things&nbsp;
like Tinder Gold that could make even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:02.080
more money from its huge user base.
But the founders claimed Match and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:06.560
IAC delayed or hid just how much&nbsp;
those new features could be worth.&nbsp;

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:12.640
And remember, this was when Tinder was absolutely&nbsp;
exploding. It felt like everyone was using it.&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:17.920
Then just hours after that valuation,&nbsp;
Match merged Tinder into the company.&nbsp;

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:24.800
And that meant all the future valuations&nbsp;
planned for 2018, 2020, and 2021 were cancelled.&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:27.760
So the founders sued.
Basically, they claimed Match&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:33.520
kept Tinder’s value low, got rid of any chance to&nbsp;
fix it later, and saved billions in the process.&nbsp;

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:38.080
And this brings us to the weird&nbsp;
revolving door of people running Tinder.&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:45.200
Greg Blatt was replaced by Elie Seidman in 2018.
Then Jim Lanzone took over in 2020.&nbsp;

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:51.200
A year later, Renate Nyborg became CEO.
Confusing? Absolutely.&nbsp;

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:56.240
But you could see the struggle for power&nbsp;
within Match Group, over this cash cow.&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:02.400
And the whole time, that lawsuit was still going.
Part of the lawsuit described IAC's controlling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:08.880
shareholder, Barry Diller, as someone who had “a&nbsp;
well-earned reputation as a notorious bully with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.920
a volcanic temper and a habit of threatening&nbsp;
to fire employees who contradicted him.”&nbsp;

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:21.360
It described Greg Blatt, Match CEO and interim&nbsp;
CEO of Tinder, as “a longtime lackey of IAC’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.680
controlling shareholder Barry Diller,”
Yikes.&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:32.560
Then in 2021, Match and IAC settled&nbsp;
the lawsuit for $441 million.&nbsp;

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:37.600
And around this same time, CEO&nbsp;
Renate Nyborg had another idea.&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:44.640
The Tinderverse. Basically, a metaverse for dating&nbsp;
with its own virtual currency called Tinder Coins.&nbsp;

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:49.760
Which… lasted for eleven months.
In August 2022, Match Group’s new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.560
CEO Bernard Kim killed both projects&nbsp;
and announced Nyborg was leaving.&nbsp;

00:08:54.560 --> 00:09:00.880
To him, Tinder "has not been able to realize the&nbsp;
monetization success that we typically deliver,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:07.360
due to "disappointing execution on several&nbsp;
optimizations and new product initiatives."&nbsp;

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:10.800
Kim then ran Tinder himself for&nbsp;
another year and a half before Faye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:17.120
Iosotaluno became CEO in January 2024.
It was a really weird time for Tinder.&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:21.840
There was constant fighting behind the&nbsp;
scenes and all these strange new ideas.&nbsp;

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:26.560
But somehow, the app was still growing.
By the third quarter of 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:31.760
11.1 million people were paying for Tinder.
And just a year earlier, its parent company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:36.160
Match Group had briefly been&nbsp;
worth more than $40 billion.&nbsp;

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:41.040
But while all of this was going on, they&nbsp;
may have missed a much bigger problem.&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:54.320
People were getting sick of playing Tinder’s game…
If you’ve made it this far, consider subscribing.&nbsp;

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:57.600
It really helps the channel and&nbsp;
you won’t miss the next video.&nbsp;

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:02.560
Tinder’s original promise was simple.&nbsp;
You never had to see rejection.&nbsp;

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.120
But when it came to actually getting&nbsp;
dates, it wasn’t working that well either.&nbsp;

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:14.400
Even with 1.6 billion swipes every day, Tinder&nbsp;
was only producing about 26 million matches.&nbsp;

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:19.600
And that created a pretty weird problem.
“Men see that they are matching with few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:24.080
people, and therefore become even less&nbsp;
discerning; women, on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:29.120
find that they match with most men, and&nbsp;
therefore become even more discerning.”&nbsp;

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:32.800
The same study found that men liked a&nbsp;
huge number of the profiles they saw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:41.120
but only matched 0.6% of the time. Women were way&nbsp;
more picky, but matched around 10% of the time.&nbsp;

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:46.640
Basically, Tinder was pushing both sides&nbsp;
into these weird “extreme strategies.”&nbsp;

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:50.400
And after a while, Tinder&nbsp;
just didn’t feel good to use.&nbsp;

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:54.400
In some ways, it almost felt&nbsp;
worse than just getting rejected.&nbsp;

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:57.840
Eventually, people started&nbsp;
getting tired of the whole game.&nbsp;

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:02.480
Dating app burnout became a real&nbsp;
problem. Especially with younger people.&nbsp;

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:07.200
Gen Z started moving more toward serious&nbsp;
dating and just meeting people in real life.&nbsp;

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:13.600
In fact, one survey of U.S. college and graduate&nbsp;
students found that 79% weren’t using any dating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:20.480
app at all. Back in 2019, only about half&nbsp;
of 18-to-29-year-olds said the same thing.&nbsp;

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:24.240
And pretty soon, Tinder’s&nbsp;
numbers started showing it.&nbsp;

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:33.040
Paying users peaked at 11.1 million in 2022. By&nbsp;
the end of 2025, that had fallen to 8.8 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:39.040
Then another 200,000 users disappeared&nbsp;
in just the first three months of 2026.&nbsp;

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:46.320
Revenue fell too. It dropped 4% in 2025&nbsp;
to $1.9 billion, marking the first yearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.200
decline in Tinder’s history.
And somehow, it gets worse.&nbsp;

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:55.520
As Tinder started to struggle,&nbsp;
activist investors showed up.&nbsp;

00:11:55.520 --> 00:12:01.440
In 2024, firms like Elliott, Anson Funds,&nbsp;
and Starboard Value started buying stakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:07.360
in Match Group, Tinder’s parent company.
And Starboard had a pretty simple question.&nbsp;

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:12.080
How do you nearly double your revenue&nbsp;
and still become less profitable?&nbsp;

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:20.480
Match made around $2 billion in revenue in 2019.&nbsp;
By 2024, that was expected to hit $3.6 billion.&nbsp;

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:27.200
But Starboard pointed out that its profit margins&nbsp;
had actually gotten worse over that same time.&nbsp;

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:36.640
Then on May 8, 2025, Match Group cut 13% of its&nbsp;
workforce. Around 325 people lost their jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:41.360
including roughly one in five managers.
The goal was to save around $100 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:44.000
a year and make investors happier.
[Spencer Rascoff, Match Group CEO] “We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:48.640
lit a fire under the team here.”
And then came another lawsuit.&nbsp;

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:54.480
A 2024 class-action lawsuit accused Tinder and&nbsp;
Hinge of purposely making dating feel like a game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.480 --> 00:13:00.800
so people would keep coming back for more matches.
Then in June 2026, eleven years after it started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:06.640
the age-pricing case finally ended with&nbsp;
a $60.5 million settlement for around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:11.200
268,000 people in California.
Of course, Tinder still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:15.120
denied doing anything wrong.
But here’s the ironic part.&nbsp;

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:19.120
Match already had the answer to Tinder’s problem.
Hinge.&nbsp;

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:23.040
The whole idea was basically the&nbsp;
opposite of Tinder. It was an app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:28.000
you were supposed to use and then delete.
It was for people who were tired of endless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:32.720
swiping and actually wanted to meet someone.
Whether that idea was completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:36.880
true or not, it worked.
Hinge’s direct revenue went from just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:45.280
$8 million in 2018 to $691 million in 2025.
Its paying users also grew 17%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:52.720
in a single year to 1.9 million.
At the same time, Tinder’s paying users fell 8%.&nbsp;

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:56.480
Hinge even limited how many&nbsp;
people you could like each day.&nbsp;

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:01.200
The whole app was built to stop you&nbsp;
from endlessly scrolling through people.&nbsp;

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:05.760
Because as McLeod put it, "[We are]&nbsp;
really explicit that this is a place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:11.360
for people who want to get off dating apps.&nbsp;
Ultimately, our community is the product."&nbsp;

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:15.600
So now, kind of ironically, Tinder&nbsp;
is trying to kill the swipe.&nbsp;

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:20.640
The company is testing Chemistry, an&nbsp;
AI feature that asks you questions and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:26.240
with your permission, looks through your camera&nbsp;
roll to figure out your interests and personality.&nbsp;

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:31.120
Rascoff called it an “AI way&nbsp;
to interact with Tinder.”&nbsp;

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:36.080
Yeah, I don’t know if I’d want an AI looking&nbsp;
through my phone to decide who I should date…&nbsp;

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:41.600
But Tinder is changing the rest of the app too.
Face Check now makes new U.S. users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:46.800
verify themselves with a video selfie.
Music Mode and Astrology Mode give people new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:51.280
ways to find matches based on what they like.
And Tinder is even going back into the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:56.480
world with things like college events.
Then in 2026, Tinder got its first major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.480 --> 00:15:00.240
rebrand in almost a decade.
Even the slogan changed.&nbsp;

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:07.680
“Happily ever after” became “happily TBD.”
So, did any of this actually work?&nbsp;

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:11.360
Can Tinder make a comeback?
Well…&nbsp;

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:16.400
In March 2026, Tinder registrations&nbsp;
finally started growing again for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:21.840
the first time in almost two years.
But the rest of the numbers weren’t as good.&nbsp;

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:28.480
Paying users still fell 5%.
Basically, fewer people were paying. But Tinder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:34.800
was making more money from each person who did.
In the first quarter of 2026, revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:41.840
per payer rose 7% to $17.56.
Then in Q2, Tinder’s revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:48.080
was basically flat, EBITDA fell 5%,&nbsp;
and paying users dropped another 6%.&nbsp;

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:54.160
Meanwhile, Hinge revenue was up 22%.
Match Group was also giving a huge amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.160 --> 00:16:02.000
of cash back to shareholders. In 2025,&nbsp;
it returned 108% of its free cash flow.&nbsp;

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:08.160
And they also announced a 13% staff cut.
Tinder was built to keep you swiping.&nbsp;

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:13.840
Hinge was built to make you delete it.
And right now, only one of them is still growing.

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:19.040
Tinder isn’t the only dating app struggling.
Bumble is dealing with a lot of the same problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:24.080
and right now, they’re all over the news.
